`/`overflow-checks` for `[profile.dev]`/`[profile.test]`.\n\n## Mutation audit (evidence the checkmarks mean something)\n\nI injected 42 mutants one at a time. **Every observable one was caught**; the 7 that weren't are provably equivalent programs — e.g. `f13`'s `min < g` → `min <= g` only changes `min`'s ±0 sign, and `min` is read only by `max - min`, which differs only when `max` is ±0, where `delta == 0` returns the same `{0,0,max}` anyway. All 7 are documented with proofs in `CONFIGS.md`.\n\nTwo changes I made to `src/lib.rs` (aligning `c2CircletoCircle`'s `addss` and `f12`'s `mulss` operand order with gcc's actual codegen) turned out to be **unobservable** — I verified this by mutation rather than assuming, and documented why.\n\n## Two corrections where I was initially wrong, not the C\n\n- `f3(-50, -49) == 2`, but `floor(-50/-49) == 1`. The C's \"floor division\" over-shoots for two negative operands. My test's `floor` reference model was wrong; I replaced it with an arm-by-arm model of the C.\n- `f11`'s `h < 120.0f && h < 180.0f` (instead of `h >= 120.0f`) is a real C typo that routes *negative* hue into band 3. Reproduced verbatim; \"fixing\" it is caught by 6 tests.\n\nOne residual risk worth flagging: Rust links `compiler-builtins`' `fmodf`/`floorf` while C imports glibc's (visible in `nm -D --undefined-only`). Both are exactly specified by IEEE-754, but NaN payloads could differ, so I added rows C71–C73 sweeping the full `f32` exponent range (~65k inputs each) through both call sites — all agree."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":744,"cache_read_input_tokens":279588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":744,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd509def-20d9-4ee5-b194-f15c3d018659","uuid":"75e115f7-383d-4b97-9862-ebeaaacd8241","timestamp":"2026-08-20T06:03:23.631Z"}
